WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.435
Lesson 11.4.

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:04.450
Stress-testing a portfolio.

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:06.789
By the end of this lesson, you'll be able

00:00:06.789 --> 00:00:09.518
to run standard shock scenarios on your book and

00:00:09.518 --> 00:00:11.635
fix what fails before markets test it.

00:00:12.935 --> 00:00:14.225
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.225 --> 00:00:15.910
First, the key ideas.

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:18.625
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:18.625 --> 00:00:20.150
Then, your checklist.

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:22.312
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:23.612 --> 00:00:28.251
Scenarios every operator runs: crypto −50% in a day

00:00:28.251 --> 00:00:32.445
· a stablecoin depegs 10% · borrow rates spike

00:00:32.445 --> 00:00:36.281
to 20% · your largest protocol is hacked ·

00:00:36.281 --> 00:00:38.990
your main L2 halts for 48h.

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:41.790
For each: loss, liquidations, liquidity, and

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:43.967
whether the payout policy survives.

00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:50.709
Holdings: ETH spot $60k · LST collateral

00:00:50.709 --> 00:00:55.620
$60k with $12k debt · stable lending

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:00.399
$50k ($25k each in two protocols) ·

00:01:00.399 --> 00:01:05.212
ETH/USDC LP $20k · PT stable $10k.

00:01:05.212 --> 00:01:12.881
ETH −50%, Spot −$30k; collateral −$30k (HF 4.0 →

00:01:12.881 --> 00:01:20.707
2.0, no liquidation); LP $20k → $14.1k (−$5.9k) →

00:01:20.707 --> 00:01:29.302
−$65.9k (−32.9%), Survives, but the payout must fall.

00:01:29.302 --> 00:01:36.338
One stablecoin −10%, −$2.5k on $25k, Yes.

00:01:36.338 --> 00:01:41.816
Borrow rate 20%, Interest on $12k rises from

00:01:41.816 --> 00:01:47.473
~$600 to $2,400/yr, Yes: covered by reserve.

00:01:47.473 --> 00:01:52.418
One lending protocol hacked (total loss),

00:01:52.418 --> 00:01:57.428
−$25k (−12.5%), Survives; cap was 12.5%.

00:01:57.428 --> 00:02:01.076
L2 halted 48h, Positions frozen; the reserve

00:02:01.076 --> 00:02:04.238
on another chain covers needs, Yes, if

00:02:04.238 --> 00:02:06.913
the reserve isn't on the same L2.

00:02:08.213 --> 00:02:09.673
Here's your checklist.

00:02:09.673 --> 00:02:11.208
Do it now, for real.

00:02:11.208 --> 00:02:14.143
First, Five scenarios run on the current book.

00:02:14.143 --> 00:02:16.370
Next, every "fail" has a fix (smaller

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:18.898
cap, bigger buffer, move the reserve).

00:02:18.898 --> 00:02:22.266
And finally, Re-run quarterly and after big changes.

00:02:23.567 --> 00:02:24.777
Question 1.

00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:28.212
Why did the collateral position survive −50%?

00:02:32.632 --> 00:02:35.522
The answer: It started at HF 4.0;

00:02:35.522 --> 00:02:38.327
halving the price took it to 2.0.

00:02:39.627 --> 00:02:40.872
Question 2.

00:02:40.872 --> 00:02:43.147
What does the L2-halt scenario test?

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:49.495
The answer: Whether your liquidity

00:02:49.495 --> 00:02:50.927
depends on a single chain.

00:02:52.227 --> 00:02:53.497
Question 3.

00:02:53.497 --> 00:02:55.272
What's the output of a stress test?

00:02:59.692 --> 00:03:04.471
The answer: Fixes: smaller caps, bigger buffers, better-placed reserves.

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:08.285
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:03:08.285 --> 00:03:10.885
Next up, Lesson 11.5: Incident

00:03:10.885 --> 00:03:13.485
response: the first 60 minutes.
